es/guan-yu/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/guan-yu/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/guan-yu/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/places/guan-yu/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Shrines & Vegetarian Halls</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Shrines and vegetarian halls</span><h1>Guan Yu / Sin Chai Tung Shrine</h1><p class="english">Guan Yu / Sin Chai Tung Shrine</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified 4 items</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="guan-yu">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">View your trip</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/guan-yu.webp" alt="Guan Yu / Sin Chai Tung Shrine" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.82945, 98.36518 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.82945,98.36518" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://mapcarta.com/W816145901" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd>Reviews mention a public toilet near the shrine, but its current availability and facilities inside have not been verified <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd>Community sources note that vegetarian festival participants wear white; no dress code requirements are found for normal days. <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9670000093105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a><a href="https://wanderlog.com/place/details/10219897/sin-chai-tueng-shrine" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#news">News and updates</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#getting_there">Getting there</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>Guan Yu faith in the mining town: The origins of the South Market Shrine</h3><p class="era-years">1843 CE (an oral account; not verified)</p><span class="fact-status disputed">Conflicting information</span><p>An account recorded by Gplace says that Hokkien Chinese people who came to mine in Khlong Pi community joined together to build a Guan Yu shrine, with Lim Bun Tuek involved in its founding, and began a nine-day, nine-night Vegetarian Festival ritual in 1843 CE. The same source spells personal names inconsistently, and no primary evidence has been found, so this should be read as an origin legend, not a verified year</p><p>At that time, Takua Pa was a coastal town growing through tin mining and Hokkien Chinese merchant networks. The shrine therefore likely served both as a home for the community's protective deity and as a ritual centre for miners and merchants. This is consistent with the municipality's description that the shrine arose from Hokkien Chinese faith and was the origin of Takua Pa's Vegetarian Festival, but the year and builder have not been verified in academic documents.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gplace</a> · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality)</a> · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/951" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Takua Pa Town</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>From a community shrine to the South Market Shrine in the old town</h3><p class="era-years">B.E. 2445 (traditional account; not verified) to before B.E. 2567</p><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p>Gplace says the shrine was moved from Khlong Pi community to a permanent site at Ban Chae Lim in Talat Yai, and that funds were raised to buy a house in 1902 CE by Lim Eng Cheng, or Tao Kae Pho Daeng. However, no evidence has been found confirming the building date or transfer of ownership, so that year should not be treated as the construction year of the current shrine</p><p>The evidence found describes continuity in the site's role rather than a change of function: the shrine remains tied to Takua Pa's Vegetarian Festival, which has been passed down among Thai people of Chinese descent on the Andaman coast, and later became part of the Talat Yai old-town landscape. At the same time, no online evidence has been found confirming any restoration, abandonment, direct impact from the 2004 tsunami, or registration of this shrine as a historic site.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gplace</a> · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/951" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Takua Pa Town</a> · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Talat Yai</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>A shrine still breathing with the vegetarian festival</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The latest news evidence found confirms the shrine's use as Talat Tai's vegetarian-festival shrine: on 2 October 2024, a Ko Teng pole-raising ceremony was held there to begin the Vegetarian Festival. The hanging of nine lanterns and continuing ceremonies show that the shrine remains a centre of community faith and shared activities, not merely an old building in the market area</p><p>For 2026, no source has confirmed opening hours, a custodian, admission fees, the building's latest condition, or a restoration project specifically for the shrine. Contact the community or municipality before travelling. Based on the information available, the shrine is near the junction of Thanon Udom Thara and Thanon Si Takua Pa in the Talat Yai area, and the confirmed activities are the Vegetarian Festival and the Ko Teng pole-raising ceremony.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9670000093105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Manager Online</a> · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Talat Yai</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">1843 CE (2386 BE)</p><h3>Story of the shrine's founding and the start of the vegetarian festival</h3><p>Gplace states that the Guan Yu shrine was founded and the 9-day vegetarian festival started in the Khlong Pi community, but there is no primary evidence to verify this.</p><a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">1902 CE (2445 BE)</p><h3>Story of permanent relocation to Talat Yai</h3><p>Gplace notes fundraising to buy a house to relocate the shrine to Talat Yai, but it is not verified if this was the year the current building was constructed.</p><a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">2 October 2024</p><h3>Go Teng pole raising at Rong Phra Talat Tai</h3><p>News reports the Go Teng pole raising ceremony to start the Takua Pa people's vegetarian festival.</p><a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9670000093105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Guan Yu and Guanyin statues</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Key objects of worship noted by the municipality for this shrine.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Two architectural styles</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Gplace mentions a Sino-Portuguese building and a Chinese shrine building on opposite sides of the road, though the styles are not verified by architectural surveys.</p><a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Deity palanquins</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Gplace notes palanquins used in processions, but availability for viewing should be checked on-site.</p><a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="news"><h2>News and updates</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><p class="muted">2024-10-02 · MGR Online</p><h3>An old shrine in Takua Pa district, Phang Nga province, has performed the Go Teng pole raising ceremony</h3><p>Reports on the Go Teng pole raising ceremony at Guan Yu (Sin Chai Tung) Shrine, Rong Phra Talat Tai, to begin the vegetarian festival.</p><a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9670000093105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The Takua Pa Town Municipality states that this shrine is the origin of the Takua Pa vegetarian festival.</p><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Another name used for this shrine is Rong Phra Talat Tai (South Market Shrine).</p><a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9670000093105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="getting_there"><h2>Getting there</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Takua Pa Town</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The destination is in the Talat Yai district, near the intersection of Udom Thara and Sri Takua Pa roads. No verified bus routes from the new town to the shrine have been found.</p><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Phuket</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Bus information websites list Route 436 (Phuket-Takua Pa) passing through Khok Kloi, Thai Mueang, and Khao Lak to the Takua Pa bus terminal. This does not confirm current schedules or onward travel to the shrine.</p><a href="https://gogo.town/route/phuket-takuapa-bus-436/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Khao Lak</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Khao Lak is on the Route 436 (Phuket-Takua Pa) bus line according to transport websites. Pick-up points and intermediate stops must be checked. Information on connecting from the bus terminal to the shrine has not been found.</p><a href="https://gogo.town/route/phuket-takuapa-bus-436/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li>Old Town Wall <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://mapcarta.com/W816145901" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li>Wat Na Mueang <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://mapcarta.com/W816145901" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/kue-chai/">Kue Chai Tung Shrine</a> <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></li><li>Wat Sena Nucharangsan <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://mapcarta.com/W816145901" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/pun-thao/">Pun Thao Kong A Shrine</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/rong-jae/">The Shrine in the Garden</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>The municipality states that the shrine grew from the faith of Hokkien Chinese people in Takua Pa, is hundreds of years old, and serves as a spiritual anchor through belief in Guan Yu and Guanyin. The municipality identifies this shrine as the origin of Takua Pa's Vegetarian Festival</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>The municipality lists important objects of worship at this shrine. Gplace says it has a palanquin used in processions. Check on site whether it is open for viewing on the day of travel</p></details><details><summary>How the municipality document mentions this place</summary><p>A shrine associated with belief in the deity Guan Yu and the trading community</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Takua Pa Municipality: Environmental Action Plan, document page 14</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre: Talat Yai community database</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">MGR Online: Ko Teng pole-raising ceremony, 2 October 2024</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9670000093105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Mapcarta: Sin Chai Tung Shrine</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://mapcarta.com/W816145901" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Gplace: Guan Yu Shrine, Sin Chai Tung</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Wanderlog: Sin Chai Tung Shrine</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wanderlog.com/place/details/10219897/sin-chai-tueng-shrine" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Go Go Town: Route 436, Phuket to Takua Pa</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://gogo.town/route/phuket-takuapa-bus-436/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Takua Pa Town</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/951" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre): Talat Yai</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Manager Online</th><td>News</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9670000093105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Gplace</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.gplace.com/972712" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">1672 TravelBuddy</th><td>Blog</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://1672travelbuddy.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>Coordinates are based on Mapcarta referencing OpenStreetMap, giving moderate confidence as it is unverified whether it points to the entrance or a specific building.</li><li>Wanderlog lists Bang Sai subdistrict, which differs from Gplace and the Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre's community database. Takua Pa subdistrict is used based on the latter. No building number was found.</li><li>Opening hours and admission fees are marked null as no verified announcements were found.</li><li>Phone number is null: Wanderlog shows +66 82 652 9164, but no shrine or official source verifies it as current.</li><li>Facebook is null: Gplace provides a broken link. No official website or LINE account was found.</li><li>Verified construction years and founders remain undiscovered. The years 1843 CE and 1902 CE are narratives from Gplace and should not be treated as direct building years.</li><li>Connections to the tin smelter are found only in stories and cannot directly confirm roles regarding mine workers or trade networks.</li><li>No Fine Arts Department documents or Royal Gazette announcements indicating historical site registration for this shrine have been found. Mention of Ministry of Interior registration in Gplace is not proof of historical site status.</li><li>Information on parking, ramps, door widths, steps, or wheelchair-accessible restrooms was not found, so accessibility for the elderly is not evaluated.</li><li>Recommended visiting times for general viewing and verified visit durations were not found.</li><li>Distances from origins to the shrine, transit routes, and walking times to nearby places are unverified. Nearby listings are based on maps, not pedestrian surveys.</li><li>A verified festival schedule for 2026 CE has not been found.</li><li>Official image links with verified sources were not found.</li><li>SEO keywords are suggestions based on names and content, not actual search volume data.</li><li>The English name follows the prompt, while varying spellings exist in Gplace and Mapcarta. An official English name announced by the shrine has not been found.</li><li>There is no primary evidence for the BE founding year or the current building's construction year.</li><li>The names of founders and caretakers across different eras remain unverified.</li><li>Evidence of restoration, abandonment, or alternative uses has not been found.</li><li>Evidence that the shrine was directly affected by the 2004 CE tsunami has not been found.</li><li>Evidence of the shrine being registered as a historical site has not been found.</li><li>Opening hours, building conditions, caretakers, and the 2026 CE festival schedule remain unverified.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
