anguage": "en", "@type": "TouristAttraction", "name": "วัดคุหาภิมุข (วัดควนถ้ำ)", "url": "https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-khuha/", "image": "https://takuapa101.com/assets/wat-khuha.webp", "description": "เอกสารกล่าวถึงอุโบสถขนาดเล็กและลักษณะสถาปัตยกรรม", "address": {"@type": "PostalAddress", "addressLocality": "ตะกั่วป่า", "addressRegion": "พังงา", "addressCountry": "TH"}, "geo": {"@type": "GeoCoordinates", "latitude": 8.831526975, "longitude": 98.367913006}}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "สถานที่นี้มีประวัติความเป็นมาอย่างไร", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "อุโบสถของวัดสร้างขึ้นเมื่อ พ.ศ. 2513 โดยนางฮวดบี ฐานข้อมูล สผ. จัดวัดแห่งนี้อยู่ในประเภทวัด วัดร้าง ศาสนสถาน และระบุว่าอุโบสถมีขนาดเล็กที่สุดในประเทศไทย"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "มีจุดเด่นอะไรให้ชม", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "ฐานข้อมูล สผ. ระบุว่าเป็นอุโบสถที่มีขนาดเล็กที่สุดในประเทศไทย ภายในอุโบสถมีพระพุทธรูปหินแกะสลักเป็นภาพนูนต่ำ ด้านหลังอุโบสถมีอักขระจารึก"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "เอกสารเทศบาลกล่าวถึงที่นี่อย่างไร", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "เอกสารกล่าวถึงอุโบสถขนาดเล็กและลักษณะสถาปัตยกรรม"}}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-khuha/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/wat-khuha/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-khuha/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/wat-khuha/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/places/wat-khuha/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Temples & Pagodas</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Temples and pagodas</span><h1>Wat Khuha Phimuk (the cave hill temple)</h1><p class="english">Wat Khuha Phimuk</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified 4 items</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="wat-khuha">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">View your trip</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/wat-khuha.webp" alt="Wat Khuha Phimuk (the cave hill temple)" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.831526975, 98.367913006 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.831526975,98.367913006" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>A small ordination hall on Khuan Tham in the mining era of Takua Pa</h3><p class="era-years">1970 (2513 BE)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The clearest evidence specifying a construction year at this time is for the temple's small ordination hall, built in 1970 (2513 BE) by Mrs. Huatbi. As for the year the entire temple complex began construction and the reason for choosing the site, there are no confirming documents yet. Therefore, one should not recount history beyond the available evidence.</p><p>This temple emerged during the late era when Takua Pa still had shared memories with tin mining. The town has roots as a port community with trade exchanges among people of many nationalities. Later, the tin mining economy and the Chinese community played a significant role. However, no evidence has yet been found that the builders of the ordination hall or Wat Khuan Tham were directly connected to the governor, the mines, or the Hokkien Chinese group.</p><p>What can be seen of the site's role is its function as a community religious site, with the ordination hall serving as a center for rituals and local art. Inside is a bas-relief stone Buddha image, behind it is a character inscription, and the boundary markers (sema) around the ordination hall are shaped as Buddha images in various postures.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Samnakngan Noyobai lae Phaen Sapphayakon Thammachat lae Singwaetlom (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · Conserved Cultural Environment Sites</a> · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · History of Takua Pa</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>From a religious site to a deserted temple list: A transition with gaps</h3><p class="era-years">After 1970 (B.E. 2513) to before 2026 (B.E. 2569) (no year found for individual events)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>Official data from the Samnakngan Nayobai Lae Phaen Sapphayakon Thammachat Lae Singwaetlom (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) classifies Wat Khuha Phimuk (Wat Khuan Tham) in the 'temple, abandoned temple, religious site' category. Meanwhile, the central religious property register of the Samnakngan Phraphutthasatsana Haeng Chat (National Office of Buddhism) lists 'Wat Khuan Tham (abandoned)' in Takua Pa district. Thus, it can only be confirmed that the temple is recorded as abandoned in government databases; it is unknown when it became abandoned or for what reason.</p><p>Takua Pa town itself transitioned from a port and mining town. As tin mining operations gradually ceased, economic liveliness decreased, and the old town shifted to being valued as a community heritage. This context helps explain the backdrop of the town's transition, but there is still no direct evidence linking Wat Khuan Tham to the impact of the mine closures.</p><p>No evidence has been found verifying any specific year of restoration, change of ownership, other uses, the 2004 (B.E. 2547) tsunami event, or a specific historical site registration announcement by Krom Sinlapakon (Fine Arts Department) for this temple.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/2021111871cc998239d7c8b8d77aa76b771a377b151224.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานพระพุทธศาสนาแห่งชาติ (National Office of Buddhism) · National central ecclesiastical property and deserted temples registration data</a> · <a href="https://elibrary.tsri.or.th/fullP/RDG60T0046/RDG60T0046V04/RDG60T0046V04_full.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sthaban Phiphitthaphan Kanrianru Haeng Chat (National Discovery Museum Institute) / Takua Pa Old Town Information Source</a> · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · History of Takua Pa</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>Present day: An art site with a tiny ordination hall, but its operational status is unclear</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>In 2026 (B.E. 2569), discovered official sources still record this place as an "abandoned temple" and do not provide opening hours, admission fees, caretakers, or contact channels. Therefore, it cannot be verified whether the temple is open to visitors or if monks reside there.</p><p>A verified highlight is the very small ordination hall, which ONEP identifies as the smallest in Thailand. Inside, there is a low-relief carved stone Buddha image with an inscription on the back, and an ancient hermit statue in the front.</p><p>No news or public announcements have been found verifying restoration projects, developments, traditional activities, or community use of the area over the past 5 years. Therefore, the current condition of the building and regular activities should be checked with locals or area caretakers before being published as travel information.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Samnakngan Noyobai lae Phaen Sapphayakon Thammachat lae Singwaetlom (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · Conserved Cultural Environment Sites</a> · <a href="https://onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/2021111871cc998239d7c8b8d77aa76b771a377b151224.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานพระพุทธศาสนาแห่งชาติ (National Office of Buddhism) · National central ecclesiastical property and deserted temples registration data</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1970 (B.E. 2513)</p><h3>Construction of the Small Ordination Hall</h3><p>The ordination hall was built by Mrs. Huatbi, featuring a carved stone Buddha image, an inscription, and boundary stones (sema) shaped like Buddha images.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>Temple Recorded as Abandoned</h3><p>The ONEP database and the National Office of Buddhism's registry list Wat Khuan Tham as an abandoned temple, though the year it was abandoned is not verified.</p><a href="https://onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/2021111871cc998239d7c8b8d77aa76b771a377b151224.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status unverified">Not verified</span><p class="era-years">Year unspecified</p><h3>No Evidence of Historical Site Registration Found</h3><p>This search did not find a Fine Arts Department registration announcement specifically confirming Wat Khuha Phimuk in Takua Pa.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Small ordination hall</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The ONEP database identifies it as the smallest ordination hall in Thailand.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Carved stone Buddha</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>Inside the ordination hall is a low-relief carved stone Buddha.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Inscription behind the ordination hall</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>There are inscribed characters behind the ordination hall.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Buddha-shaped boundary markers</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The boundary markers around the hall are shaped like Buddha images in various postures.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Ancient hermit statue</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>An ancient hermit statue stands at the front of the temple.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>ONEP notes that the ordination hall of Wat Khuha Phimuk is the smallest in Thailand.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>There is an inscription behind the ordination hall, and the boundary markers around it are shaped like Buddha images in various postures.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li><a href="/en/places/wat-boromthat/">Wat Boromthat Khiri Khet (the town's relic temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-pathum/">Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-kongkha/">Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-nikorn/">Wat Nikornwararam</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-sena/">Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>The temple's ordination hall was built in 1970 (B.E. 2513) by Mrs. Huat Bi. The ONEP database categorises this temple as a temple, an abandoned temple, and a religious site, stating that the ordination hall is the smallest in Thailand.</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>The ONEP database states that it is the smallest ordination hall in Thailand. Inside the ordination hall, there is a low-relief carved stone Buddha image. Behind the ordination hall, there are inscribed characters.</p></details><details><summary>How the municipality document mentions this place</summary><p>Documents mention the small ordination hall and its architectural characteristics.</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) (ONEP) · Conserved Cultural Art Sites</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">สำนักงานพระพุทธศาสนาแห่งชาติ (National Office of Buddhism) · National central ecclesiastical property and deserted temples registration data</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/2021111871cc998239d7c8b8d77aa76b771a377b151224.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Samnakngan Noyobai lae Phaen Sapphayakon Thammachat lae Singwaetlom (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · Conserved Cultural Environment Sites</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4415" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · History of Takua Pa</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Takua Pa Old Town Community Research Report</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://elibrary.tsri.or.th/fullP/RDG60T0046/RDG60T0046V04/RDG60T0046V04_full.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>House numbers, streets, and postal codes are not verified from accessible sources.</li><li>Opening hours, admission fees, and recommended visit durations are not verified.</li><li>Phone numbers, Facebook pages, websites, or LINE accounts of the temple are not verified.</li><li>Verified travel methods and distances from Takua Pa town, Phuket, Khao Lak, or the airport are not verified.</li><li>Parking, restrooms, and accessibility for the elderly/wheelchairs are not verified.</li><li>Festivals or traditions linked to the temple from accessible sources are not verified.</li><li>Nearby places confirmed to be within walking distance are not verified.</li><li>Official image links for the location are not verified.</li><li>Official or academic sources confirming its role in the tin mining era, Sino-Portuguese/Chinese architecture, or historical site registration are not verified.</li><li>The founding year, founder, reasons for construction, and specific roles during the era of town rulers/tin mines/maritime trade are not verified.</li><li>The year it became deserted, the cause of abandonment, ownership changes, or other uses are not verified.</li><li>Specific evidence for the temple regarding the impact of the 2004 tsunami is not verified.</li><li>Years and details of renovations are not verified.</li><li>Announcements confirming historical site registration by the Fine Arts Department are not verified.</li><li>Current caretakers, opening hours, admission fees, community activities, and development projects in 2026 are not verified.</li><li>News from the past 5 years directly related to this temple is not verified.</li><li>URLs of old or current photos confirmed to be Wat Khuha Phimuk in Takua Pa are not verified. Do not mix photos of Wat Khuha Phimuk in Yala or Wat Kongkha Phimuk in Takua Pa.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
D:\Siwaracafeweb\output\web\takuapa101-v1\site\en\places\wat-kongkha\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Wat Kongkha Phimuk · Wat Khongkha Takua Pa · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="Observing southern ordination hall lines · Khongkha Phimuk Temple · Takua Pa ancient temple · Phang Nga temple"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Wat Kongkha Phimuk · Wat Khongkha Takua Pa"><meta property="og:description" content="Observing southern ordination hall lines · Khongkha Phimuk Temple · Takua Pa ancient temple · Phang Nga temple"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-kongkha/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/wat-kongkha.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Takua Pa 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/wat-kongkha.png"><meta name="generator" content="Takua Pa 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/wat-kongkha/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "หน้าแรก", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "วัดคงคาภิมุข (วัดคงคา) · วัดคงคาพิมุข", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-kongkha/"}]}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "TouristAttraction", "name": "วัดคงคาภิมุข (วัดคงคา)", "url": "https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-kongkha/", "image": "https://takuapa101.com/assets/wat-kongkha.webp", "description": "มีอุโบสถแบบปักษ์ใต้ซึ่งเอกสารระบุว่าเป็นโบราณสถาน", "address": {"@type": "PostalAddress", "streetAddress": "ถนนราษฎร์บำรุง", "addressLocality": "ตะกั่วป่า", "addressRegion": "พังงา", "addressCountry": "TH"}, "geo": {"@type": "GeoCoordinates", "latitude": 8.840837, "longitude": 98.362899}, "telephone": "076-421-479"}, {"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "เดินทางจากตัวเมืองตะกั่วป่าอย่างไร", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "จากที่ทำการเทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่าไปทางตะวันตกตามถนนเพชรเกษม/ทางหลวงหมายเลข 4 ราว 5 กิโลเมตร เลี้ยวซ้ายเข้าถนนราษฎร์บำรุง ตรงต่อประมาณ 280 เมตร แล้วเลี้ยวซ้ายตามทาง จะพบวัดอยู่ด้านขวามือ"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "สถานที่นี้มีประวัติความเป็นมาอย่างไร", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "อุโบสถและเจดีย์เป็นโบราณสถานที่ยังใช้งานอยู่ และวัดเป็นสถานปฏิบัติธรรมประจำจังหวัดพังงาแห่งที่ 1 กรมศิลปากรประกาศขึ้นทะเบียนโบราณสถานและกำหนดเขตที่ดินในราชกิจจานุเบกษาเมื่อ 18 ธันวาคม พ.ศ. 2539"}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "มีจุดเด่นอะไรให้ชม", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "ชมอาคารก่ออิฐถือปูน หน้าบันและซุ้มประตูหน้าต่างประดับปูนปั้น รวมถึงภาพพระพุทธรูปและลวดลายพันธุ์พฤกษา เป็นหนึ่งในโบราณสถานสำคัญภายในวัดและยังอยู่ในวัดที่มีการใช้งาน วัดตั้งอยู่ริมฝั่งคลองตะกั่วป่าด้านตะวันตก โดยคลองอยู่ทางทิศตะวันออกของวัด"}}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-kongkha/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/places/wat-kongkha/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/places/wat-kongkha/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/" aria-current="page">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/places/wat-kongkha/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/places/wat-kongkha/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/places/">Places</a> / Temples & Pagodas</div><header class="place-hero full-place-hero"><div><span class="chapter">Place record · Temples and pagodas</span><h1>Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</h1><p class="english">Wat Kongkha Phimuk</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">6 items verified</span><p><a class="text-link" href="#eras">Then · Before · Now ↓</a></p><p class="place-hero-actions"><button class="button" type="button" data-trip-add="wat-kongkha">Add to trip</button><a class="text-link" href="/en/trip/">View your trip</a></p></div><div class="place-hero-art"><img src="/assets/wat-kongkha.webp" alt="Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)" width="1000" height="1000" loading="eager"></div></header><div class="research-layout"><aside class="visit-card" id="visit"><h2>Practical information</h2><dl><div><dt>Location</dt><dd>8.840837, 98.362899 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div class="directions"><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=8.840837,98.362899" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navigate with Google Maps ↗</a><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coordinates source ↗</a></div><div><dt>Opening hours</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Admission fee</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Parking</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Restrooms</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Wheelchair access</dt><dd>OpenStreetMap data via Mapcarta indicates a wheelchair=yes tag, but details on ramp conditions or facilities from official sources are not verified. <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Dress code</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Phone</dt><dd>076-421-479 <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Page</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>Website</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><dt>LINE</dt><dd><span class="muted">Not verified</span></dd></div><div><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a><a href="https://mapcarta.com/29028704" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Visitor information sources ↗</a><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Contact information ↗</a></div></dl></aside><nav class="research-rail" aria-label="สารบัญหน้านี้"><span class="chapter">On this page</span><a href="#visit">Practical information</a><a href="#eras">Then · Before · Now</a><a href="#timeline">Dot timeline</a><a href="#highlights">Highlights to see</a><a href="#did_you_know">Did you know</a><a href="#getting_there">Getting there</a><a href="#nearby">Nearby and similar places</a><a href="#faq">FAQ</a><a href="#sources">Data Sources</a></nav><div class="research-body"><section class="research-section" id="eras"><h2>Then · Before · Now</h2><div class="era-axis"><article class="era-card" data-era="then"><span class="chapter">Then</span><h3>A riverside temple of Takua Pa, starting under the name 'Wat Kongkha'</h3><p class="era-years">1762 (B.E. 2305) (proposed by local and government sources); 1936 (B.E. 2479) (ordination hall)</p><span class="fact-status disputed">Conflicting information</span><p>Evidence from the environmental art agency states the temple was built in 1762 (B.E. 2305) and originally named Wat “Kongkha”, while the archaeological database of Sun Manutsayawitthaya Sirindhorn (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) indicates the age of the important historical site based on the ordination hall built in 1936 (B.E. 2479). The origin should therefore be read as a two-layer history: the temple's age according to registration or accounts, and the age of the surviving buildings, which are not the same.</p><p>The temple is located on an alluvial plain along Khlong Takua Pa, on the west side of the waterway, in a town with a history as a coastal city and a centre for tin mining and maritime trade. This urban context suggests the temple likely served as a religious site for the riverside community. However, no specific evidence for the temple has been found identifying the founder, the reason for construction, or the direct role of Hokkien Chinese or the town governor. Therefore, details beyond the evidence should not be added.</p><p>The ordination hall, built in 1936 (B.E. 2479), is a southern-style brick-and-mortar building facing east, decorated with stucco depicting pagodas, Buddha images, Dharma wheels, and flora. This reflects its role as both a space for religious ceremonies and a showcase of community craftsmanship from the late Rattanakosin period to the reign of King Rama VIII.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4417" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cultural Art Environment (ONEP)</a> · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ฐานข้อมูลแหล่งโบราณคดีที่สำคัญในประเทศไทย (ศมส.) (Important Archaeological Sites in Thailand Database, SAC)</a> · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality) Environmental Operation Plan</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="before"><span class="chapter">Before</span><h3>A new name, a new historical site, and returning as a living temple</h3><p class="era-years">1940 (B.E. 2483); 1996 (B.E. 2539); 2002 (B.E. 2545)</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The temple history recorded in the archaeological database states that it was originally called Wat Kongkha before being changed to Wat Kongkha Phimuk in 1940 (B.E. 2483). Meanwhile, the ordination hall and pagoda are still in use and have not become ruins isolated from the temple. The different names in documents should be preserved to show readers the site's development.</p><p>Krom Sinlapakon (Fine Arts Department) announced the registration of the ordination hall and pagoda, along with designating the historical site boundaries, in the Royal Gazette on 18 December 1996 (B.E. 2539). The registration ensures the heritage value of the old buildings is protected as an artistic legacy. Concurrently, it remains a private temple affiliated with the Maha Nikaya sect, with resident monks.</p><p>The provincial dharma practice centre registry of Samnakngan Phraphutthasatsana Haeng Chat (National Office of Buddhism) lists “Kongkha Phimuk” as the 1st dharma practice centre of Phang Nga province according to a 2002 (B.E. 2545) resolution. Currently, the archaeological database indicates that it is maintained and generally in stable condition. However, no evidence has been found verifying if the area was affected by the 2004 (B.E. 2547) tsunami or if it had a specific period of abandonment or restoration.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ฐานข้อมูลแหล่งโบราณคดีที่สำคัญในประเทศไทย (ศมส.) (Important Archaeological Sites in Thailand Database, SAC)</a> · <a href="https://www.onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/20230608fc490ca45c00b1249bbe3554a4fdf6fb104517.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานพระพุทธศาสนาแห่งชาติ (National Office of Buddhism) Provincial Meditation Center Registry</a></div></article><article class="era-card" data-era="now"><span class="chapter">Now</span><h3>A historical site that still breathes: An active temple and meditation center</h3><p class="era-years">2569 / 2026</p><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>In the latest published information, Wat Kongkha Phimuk still has resident monks and operates as a private temple affiliated with the Maha Nikaya sect. The maintaining agency is stated to be the temple itself, with the archaeological database advising to contact Phra Khru Nanthapanyakon, the abbot, at 076-421-479.</p><p>The ordination hall and pagoda are active historical sites. The temple interior is described as clean, shady, and suitable for religious practices. The temple also serves as the 1st provincial dharma practice centre of Phang Nga, making it a place where people can pay respects to Buddha, admire the stucco work, and engage in dharma practice.</p><p>Activities with cited sources include dharma practice in honour of the monarchy, overnight New Year prayers, Brahmin nun ordination for royal merit, and summer novice ordination. However, no 2026 (B.E. 2569) announcements have been found verifying activity schedules, opening hours, new restoration projects, or facility details. Therefore, you should call the temple before travelling.</p><div class="era-sources">References · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ฐานข้อมูลแหล่งโบราณคดีที่สำคัญในประเทศไทย (ศมส.) (Important Archaeological Sites in Thailand Database, SAC)</a> · <a href="https://www.onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/20230608fc490ca45c00b1249bbe3554a4fdf6fb104517.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">สำนักงานพระพุทธศาสนาแห่งชาติ (National Office of Buddhism) Provincial Meditation Center Registry</a></div></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="timeline"><h2>Dot timeline</h2><ol class="point-timeline" tabindex="0" aria-label="เส้นเวลา เลื่อนเพื่ออ่านเหตุการณ์"><li><span class="fact-status disputed">Conflicting information</span><p class="era-years">1762 (B.E. 2305)</p><h3>Founding of Wat Kongkha (Data conflicts with building age evidence)</h3><p>ONEP states the temple was built in 1762 and originally named Wat Kongkha, but the archaeological database uses 1936 as the age of the ubosot, so it cannot be concluded whether the site and the building started in the same year.</p><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4417" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">1936 (B.E. 2479)</p><h3>Construction of the ubosot which is an important historical site</h3><p>The rectangular brick and mortar ubosot faces east and features stucco designs and southern Thai style roof structures.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status disputed">Conflicting information</span><p class="era-years">1940 (B.E. 2483)</p><h3>Renamed to Wat Kongkha Phimuk</h3><p>The temple history in the archaeological database states that it was originally named Wat Kongkha and then renamed to Wat Kongkha Phimuk.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">18 December 1996 (2539)</p><h3>Registered as a historical site</h3><p>The Fine Arts Department announced the registration of the historical site and defined the land area in the Royal Gazette Volume 113, Special Part 50 Ngor.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p class="era-years">2002 (B.E. 2545)</p><h3>Became the first provincial meditation practice center of Phang Nga</h3><p>The National Office of Buddhism registry lists Wat Kongkha Phimuk as the first center of Phang Nga province according to resolution 12/2002.</p><a href="https://www.onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/20230608fc490ca45c00b1249bbe3554a4fdf6fb104517.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li></ol></section><p class="missing-photo">No image authorized for publication yet</p><section class="research-section" id="highlights"><h2>Highlights to see</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Historical site ordination hall</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>View the brick and stucco building. The gable and door or window arches are decorated with stucco art, including Buddha images and floral patterns.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Pagoda</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>It is one of the important historical sites within the temple and remains part of the active temple.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Atmosphere along the Takua Pa Canal</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The temple is located on the western bank of the Takua Pa Canal, with the canal situated to the east of the temple.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Stucco details on the gable</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>The front features a pagoda, Buddha images in the gazing and alms-bowl holding postures, while the back features the Dharmachakra and a Naga-protected Buddha.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="did_you_know"><h2>Did you know</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The name 'Kongkha Phimuk' appears as the first entry in the Phang Nga provincial meditation practice center register.</p><a href="https://www.onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/20230608fc490ca45c00b1249bbe3554a4fdf6fb104517.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><span class="fact-status confirmed">Verified</span><p>The ordination hall and pagoda still serve as active historical sites and are not closed off merely as archaeological viewing spots.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="getting_there"><h2>Getting there</h2><div class="research-cards"><article class="research-note"><h3>Takua Pa Town</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span><p>From the Takua Pa Town Municipality office, head west along Phetkasem Road / Highway 4 for about 5 kilometers, turn left onto Rat Bamrung Road, go straight for about 280 meters, then turn left along the path. The temple will be on the right.</p><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Phuket</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Khao Lak</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Airport</h3><span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span></article></div></section><section class="research-section" id="nearby"><h2>Nearby and similar places</h2><ul class="nearby-links"><li>Somchai Andaman Building <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://mapcarta.com/29028704" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li>Kanda Supermarket <span class="fact-status unknown">Not verified</span> <a href="https://mapcarta.com/29028704" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read source ↗</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-boromthat/">Wat Boromthat Khiri Khet (the town's relic temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-khuha/">Wat Khuha Phimuk (the cave hill temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-pathum/">Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-nikorn/">Wat Nikornwararam</a></li><li><a href="/en/places/wat-sena/">Wat Sena Nuch Rangsan</a></li></ul></section><section class="research-section" id="faq"><h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>Getting there from Takua Pa Town</summary><p>From the Takua Pa Town Municipality office, head west along Phetkasem Road / Highway 4 for about 5 kilometers, turn left onto Rat Bamrung Road, go straight for about 280 meters, then turn left along the path. The temple will be on the right.</p></details><details><summary>What is the history of this place?</summary><p>The ordination hall and pagoda are active historical sites, and the temple serves as the first provincial meditation practice center of Phang Nga. The Fine Arts Department (Krom Sinlapakon) announced its registration as a historical site and defined its land boundaries in the Royal Gazette on December 18, 1996 (2539 BE).</p></details><details><summary>What are the highlights to see?</summary><p>Observe the brick and mortar building, with its pediment and arched doors and windows decorated with stucco, including Buddha images and floral patterns. It is one of the important historical sites within the temple and is still in an active temple. The temple is located on the west bank of Khlong Takua Pa, with the canal situated to the east of the temple.</p></details></section><section class="research-section" id="sources"><h2>Data Sources</h2><div class="source-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Type</th><th>Date accessed</th><th>Link</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">ฐานข้อมูลแหล่งโบราณคดีที่สำคัญในประเทศไทย (ศมส.) (Important Archaeological Sites in Thailand Database, SAC)</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Cultural Art Environment (ONEP)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4417" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Mapcarta (Data from OpenStreetMap)</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://mapcarta.com/29028704" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">TAT Media Assets</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://tatmediaassets.com/file/1097442" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Thailand YellowPages</th><td>Other resources</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.yellowpages.co.th/profile/%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%87%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A0%E0%B8%B4%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%82-nabHw5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Samnakngan Phraphutthasatsana Haeng Chat (National Office of Buddhism)</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.onab.go.th/th/file/get/file/20230608fc490ca45c00b1249bbe3554a4fdf6fb104517.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality) Environmental Operation Plan</th><td>Official agency</td><td>2026-09-07</td><td><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_news_formdoc/2021-08_35dfd0d7b952500.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class="unknowns"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>Published and verified opening hours are not verified.</li><li>Admission fee data is not verified.</li><li>Facebook pages, websites, or LINE accounts confirmed to belong to the temple are not verified.</li><li>A postal code verified to match the temple from primary sources is not verified.</li><li>Routes and distances that can be verified from Phuket, Khao Lak, or the airport are not verified.</li><li>Data confirming the temple's role in the tin mining era is not verified.</li><li>Evidence confirming Sino-Portuguese or Chinese architecture is not verified.</li><li>Data confirming parking, restrooms, and recommended visit times are not verified.</li><li>Months or lunar calendar dates for the festivals are not verified.</li><li>Walking times to nearby places are not verified.</li><li>Evidence identifying the founder and reason for construction in 1762 is not verified.</li><li>Sources provide different years for the temple's start and the ordination hall's construction, so the temple's age cannot be summarized into a single year.</li><li>Evidence specifically for the temple regarding its role in tin mining, maritime trade, Hokkien Chinese, or the town ruler system is not verified.</li><li>Evidence confirming it was abandoned or damaged by the 2004 tsunami is not verified.</li><li>News from the past 5 years confirming renovation or development projects specifically for the temple are not verified.</li><li>Old photographs with verifiable sources are not verified.</li><li>A 2026 announcement regarding opening hours, activity schedules, and facilities is not verified.</li></ul></details></section></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
