"item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/architecture/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/architecture/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/architecture/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/architecture/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/architecture/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/stories/architecture/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>Old houses and architecture</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa stories · Old houses and architecture</span><h1>Buildings built with tin money and dredger steel · Architecture that remembers the town</h1><p class="lead">Read Takua Pa through the shophouses on Culture Street, Boon Sung Iron Bridge, the governor's residence, and Tao Ming School · The transition embedded in brick, mortar, and steel structures</p><p class="story-meta">Updated on 2026-09-07 · 6-minute read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/tao-ming-school.jpg" width="1477" height="1108" alt="Tao Ming School seen from the front, on the old town culture street" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Tao Ming School seen from the front, on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>If you walk through the Thanon Udom Thara and Thanon Si Takua Pa areas today, the first sight is rows of Sino-Portuguese shophouses. The front walkway has a roof cover known as &#x27;Ngo Ka Ki&#x27; which provides shade and rain protection for pedestrians. It feels like stepping back in time to a well-preserved past.</p><p>But these buildings are not just beautiful backdrops for photography. They are a massive archive recording the changes of Takua Pa in the form of brick, mortar, and steel structures. Each piece of architecture tells the story of a town that grew and prospered through tin mining, water trade, and the blending of people from diverse cultures.</p><p>To deeply understand Takua Pa's architecture, we must look beyond its external beauty. We need to distinguish between what is &#x27;newly built&#x27; and what is &#x27;repaired&#x27;, and understand the layers of history hidden within the governor's residence walls, the miners' iron bridge, and the school built from merchants' donations.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">The governor's residence and the wall that tells of conflict</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/culture-street.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>Back in the era when Takua Pa was growing from the tin mining economy and coastal trade, wealth came with conflicts of interest. The wall of the Takua Pa governor's residence was built to act like a camp wall, protecting against danger and serving as a refuge for townspeople. Scholars suggest, though not settled, that this wall was built during the time when Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon) was the governor between 1840 (2383 BE) and 1881.</p><p>The wall was constructed with plaster mixed with gravel and sand, layered with a base wider than the wall itself for stability. The part that remains today is about 5 meters high. The inner area was once divided into a front palace, a main hall, and a rear palace, reflecting the systematic space allocation of the town's rulers in that era.</p><p>Important evidence confirming the prosperity of trade in that era comes from the archaeological excavation by the Fine Arts Department in 1999, which found Qing dynasty Chinese ceramics, European ceramics, glassware, and building decorative parts. These items confirm that this governor's residence was once connected to a global trade network.</p><p>When the city center moved to Ko Kho Khao and Ban Yan Yao, the former governor's residence lost its administrative role. However, today this area has become an open archaeological site that the community uses for cultural events, such as the &#x27;From Takola to Takua Pa&#x27; festival, bringing the old walls back to life as the city's stage.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">The year the wall construction began</span></div></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Architecture is not just a beautiful backdrop. It is an archive recording the changes of Takua Pa in the form of brick, mortar, and steel structures.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">The district chief's house in the transition era</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/governor-wall.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Governor’s Residence Wall" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Governor’s Residence Wall</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The transition from the governor system to the district system was recorded in the architecture of &#x27;Ban Khun In&#x27;. According to local evidence, this house was built around 1917 (B.E. 2460) by Chinese craftsmen to be the residence of Lieutenant Khun Intharakhiri (Choi na Nakhon), a descendant of the governor lineage who later became the Takua Pa district chief.</p><p>The building is a two-story brick and masonry structure in an applied Sino-Portuguese style, reflecting the architectural influence from port cities like Penang and Singapore that spread through maritime trade. This house therefore not only displays the status of its owner but also serves as evidence showing Takua Pa's connection to the outside world.</p><p>In reading history from architecture, we must be careful to distinguish between the year of construction and the year of repair. A report from the Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning indicates that the Na Nakhon family repaired this house in 1981, trying to preserve the original style but changing the roofing material to corrugated tiles.</p><p>Knowing this repair information helps us see Ban Khun In not just as a static past, but as a living structure that has been maintained and improved by its residents over many decades.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">The year Ban Khun In was built (1917)</span></div></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>From an industrial structure, the Boon Sung Iron Bridge has changed its role to a community thoroughfare and has become a major landmark attracting people to visit the traces of the mining town.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">Educational funds from mining and merchant networks</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/iron-bridge.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Boon Soong Iron Bridge" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Boon Soong Iron Bridge</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The wealth from tin mining was not only spent on building private residences, but also led to the creation of community institutions. The &#x27;Tao Ming School&#x27; building, constructed in 1922 (B.E. 2465), is a clear example of community collaboration.</p><p>Funds for constructing this school came from fundraising among wealthy miners and Chinese merchants from the Old Town market, Yan Yao, Ranong, and Phuket. This shows the strong geographical and economic network of the Chinese community in this region.</p><p>The school building was built by Chinese craftsmen in an applied Sino-Portuguese style and operated as a primary school for a long time. It served as a center for passing on knowledge, language, and culture to generations of Thai-Chinese children in Takua Pa.</p><p>Even though the school has ceased its teaching operations today, the building is still maintained by the Takua Pa Tao Ming School Foundation, standing as a memorial to the community's commitment to education during the town's golden era.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">From a tin dredger to a workers' bridge</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/khun-in.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Khun In House" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Khun In House</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>While residences and schools reflect the wealth of merchants, the &#x27;Saphan Lek Bun Sung&#x27; (Bun Sung Iron Bridge) tells the story of the industrial era and the working class. This bridge was built in 1968 (B.E. 2511) and is a direct product of the city's tin mining industry.</p><p>Before the bridge existed, miners and villagers had to rely on rowboats to cross the Takua Pa Canal, which is over 40 meters wide, making it extremely difficult during the flood season. Although a wooden bridge for pedestrians and bicycles had been built, as the industry expanded, the demand for a stronger structure increased.</p><p>The Juti Boon Sung Dredging Company initiated the construction of this bridge by assembling the steel frames from a decommissioned dredger to form the Khok Khanun iron bridge, which is over 200 meters long. This bridge became an important shortcut connecting the Old Town market area to the mining area, facilitating labor and transportation.</p><p>Although the sound of mining machinery has long gone silent today, the iron bridge remains. It has changed its role from an industrial structure to a community thoroughfare and has become a major landmark that attracts people to visit the traces of the Takua Pa mining town.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">Culture Street and shophouses brought back to life</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/shophouse-78.webp" width="400" height="533" alt="Shophouse number 78 on the old town culture street" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Shophouse number 78 on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><p>The shophouses along both sides of Udom Tara and Sri Takua Pa streets are the town's most prominent architectural heritage. These buildings were built to align with the commercial lifestyle, featuring ground-floor spaces for business and connected Ngo Ka Ki walkways. These buildings were influenced by Straits colonial architecture, reflecting the trade networks with Penang and Singapore.</p><p>After the tin mining industry declined and the town's commercial center moved away, this Old Town market area faced quietness. Many buildings were left to decay over time, as if the golden era of this street had passed.</p><p>However, the surviving architecture has become an important asset in revitalizing this area. Around 2007 (B.E. 2550), the &#x27;Takua Pa Old Town Reminiscing&#x27; project was initiated, piloting the opening of the street space as a cultural walking street before developing into the current Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street.</p><p>Every Sunday, this street is closed to traffic and opens as a bustling market. The old Sino-Portuguese buildings return to their role as a backdrop for community activities, including the selling of local food, crafts, and performances, proving that past architecture can find new life and roles in the present day.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-disputed">The year the walking street project started</span></div></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="culture-street">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Governor’s Residence Wall</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="governor-wall">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Boon Soong Iron Bridge</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="iron-bridge">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Khun In House</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="khun-in">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Tao Ming School</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="tao-ming">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><aside class="story-outro"><p>Most of the buildings in this article can only be viewed from the outside · One teak house in the old market district has been turned into a cafe, making it one of the few buildings where you can actually go inside to see the wooden structure, ceiling, and interior openings for natural light</p><a class="text-link" href="https://siwaracafe.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The teak house you can enter ↗</a></aside><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The exact years of construction for Ban Khun In and the Takua Pa governor's residence wall</li><li>Documentary information on the later structural repair and restoration of the Boon Sung Iron Bridge</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>Old town boundary demarcation project · Takua Pa Old Town</cite> · Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning (ONEP) · PDF pages 55 to 58 · <a href="https://nced.onep.go.th/opendata/2568/oldtown36_announce/takuapa.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Tao Ming School building</cite> · สผ. (ONEP) Cultural Art Environment Database · Significance/Characteristics section · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4420" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Khun In building</cite> · สผ. (ONEP) Cultural Art Environment Database · Significance/Characteristics section · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4423" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Governor’s Residence Wall</cite> · Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre (public organisation) · Archaeological highlights · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Thanon Watthanatham Mueang Kao Takua Pa</cite> · Department of Internal Trade · Market records · <span class="source-offline">View sources <small>Original source offline · checked 8 Sep 2026</small></span> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">The year Ban Khun In was built</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">Local information states it was built in 1917, but the ONEP report and official databases do not specify a clear construction year. Therefore, primary evidence is needed for confirmation. <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4423" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The year Culture Street started</div><div class="ev-status disputed">DISPUTED</div><div class="ev-explain">The Department of Internal Trade's market registry states the founding or promotion date as 17 December 2017, but news sources and research indicate activities began since 2007. It may therefore be different milestones between event organization and registration. <span class="source-offline">References ↗ <small>Original source offline · checked 8 Sep 2026</small></span></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The year the governor's residence wall was built</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">Scholars suggest, though not settled, that it was built during the time of Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch) between 1840 and 1881, but no evidence specifying the exact year construction began has yet been found. <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">The governor's residence and the wall that tells of conflict</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">The district chief's house in the transition era</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">Educational funds from mining and merchant networks</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">From a tin dredger to a workers' bridge</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">Culture Street and shophouses brought back to life</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
D:\Siwaracafeweb\output\web\takuapa101-v1\site\en\stories\city\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>History of Takua Pa: The Town That Moved · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="From an ancient settlement on the trans-peninsular route to a market and mining town · Verified facts and questions waiting to be answered."><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="History of Takua Pa: The Town That Moved"><meta property="og:description" content="From an ancient settlement on the trans-peninsular route to a market and mining town · Verified facts and questions waiting to be answered."><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Takua Pa 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="Takua Pa 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/city/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "The History of Takua Pa, a Town That Moved with the River", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/city/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/city/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/city/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/stories/city/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>Know the city</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa Stories · About the Town</span><h1>Takua Pa: The Town That Moved with the River</h1><p class="lead">From an ancient settlement on the trans-peninsular route to a market and mining town · Verified facts and questions waiting to be answered.</p><p class="story-meta">Updated on 2026-09-07 · 6-minute read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2 is-illustration"><img src="/assets/riverwalk.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Takuapa Riverwalk" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Takuapa Riverwalk</span><span class="credit">Illustration · No licensed photo available for this subject yet</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>If you have a chance to take a walk in Takua Pa old town on a holiday afternoon, you will see old houses, long streets, and a quiet, charming atmosphere. But deep beneath the cracks in the old walls and the slow-flowing river, this town hides a story of major transformation.</p><p>Takua Pa was not located here from the beginning, and it did not always look like this. The town is a living entity that grew, expanded, and moved in rhythm with its resources and the river. From a stopover point on the trans-peninsular route, it became a bustling major market town, and later a town that had to find a new home when the river was no longer favorable.</p><p>We will take you to read Takua Pa through what remains: the governor's residence wall that tells a story of wealth, the old temple by the canal, and the river that was once its main artery. This is a town with more than one version of history.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">The Ongoing Debate Over the Town's Name</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/wat-kongkha.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>When speaking of Takua Pa, the first question many people wonder is where the town's name came from. Official sources and historians still offer different explanations, and to date, there is no evidence that can bring this to a single, settled conclusion.</p><p>The first explanation links the town's name to the word "Takola", which appears in ancient records as a major port on the trans-peninsular route. This word is interpreted as a possible root that gradually changed in pronunciation to the current name over several centuries.</p><p>Another explanation is based on local geography and resources. It suggests that the name comes from combining the word "Takua" (which means tin, a mineral abundantly found in the area) and "Pa" (which means forest, reflecting the rich natural environment surrounding the town).</p><p>Although documents from the Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning (ONEP) list both explanations as basic information, they clearly emphasize that there is no definitive evidence to confirm either one. The town's name remains the first mystery greeting visitors, leaving us to imagine the past through different explanations.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">The origin of the town's name is not settled</span></div></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Takua Pa was not located here from the beginning, and it did not always look like this. The town is a living entity that grew, expanded, and moved in rhythm with its resources and the river.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">The Governor's Residence Wall and Town Defense</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/governor-wall.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Governor’s Residence Wall" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Governor’s Residence Wall</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The image of Takua Pa's past wealth is inextricably linked to the tin mining business. One of the structures that most clearly reflects the power and context of that era is the Takua Pa governor's residence wall, which stands as a historical witness to this day.</p><p>Scholars have suggested, not settled, that this wall was built during the time of Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon) while he was the governor between 1840 (2383 BE) and 1881. The wall surrounding the residence was designed as a defensive camp. During an era when the town faced conflicts among Chinese groups linked to the massive benefits from tin mining, the wall served as both a home for the ruler and a fortress to maintain peace.</p><p>The wall's structure is highly stable, constructed with layers of mortar mixed with gravel and sand. The base was designed to be wider than the wall itself. The parts that remain intact today reach a height of about 5 meters. Inside the wall, the area was divided into the front palace, the main hall, and the rear palace, with connecting doors well-proportioned throughout.</p><p>The significance of this governor's residence also appears in historical records when King Chulalongkorn (Rama V) came to inspect the residence on May 28, 1890 (109 RS or 2433 BE). During the excavation by the Fine Arts Department in 1999, Chinese ceramics from the Qing Dynasty and European ceramics were found, providing evidence of Takua Pa's international trade connections.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 ">The construction year is suggested, not settled, based on the governor's tenure</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">A Town That Grew with the River</h2><p>The Takua Pa River is not just a backdrop for the town, but the main artery that has nourished life and trade since the pioneering era. Official documents explain that Takua Pa old town is an ancient settlement located by the river, and it prospered as a trade center on the trans-peninsular route.</p><p>The bustling water trade and tin mining led to the expansion of an area called Talat Yai (the big market) during the reign of King Rama III, which became an important economic center. Near the hustle and bustle of trade, Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the town's temple) or Wat Kongkha, is situated on the west bank of the Takua Pa canal, serving as the spiritual center for the townspeople.</p><p>The history of Wat Kongkha reflects the complexity of the old town's information. The cultural environment data states that this temple was built in 1762, while the archaeological database uses 1936 as the construction year for the beautiful southern style ordination hall. This hall is decorated with stucco floral patterns and Buddha images, which are still preserved today.</p><p>The differences in these sets of numbers are something we often find in local history. Explaining the town's antiquity through official documents only provides an overview of the settlement in the entire area. It does not mean that every building, every bridge, or every location in the present town has the same age dating back to the town's founding era.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 ">The age of the location and the age of the building might not be the same year</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">The Day the River Grew Shallow and the Town Had to Move</h2><p>But nature never stays still, and the river is always ready to change. As time passed, the river that built the town began to change its role. ONEP reports note the end of the golden age of Talat Yai, which was primarily caused by geography and changes in resources.</p><p>Long and continuous tin mining caused a massive amount of sand and sediment to flow down and accumulate in the river. The Takua Pa River, which used to be deep enough for cargo ships and passenger boats to navigate easily, gradually became shallower until large-scale navigation declined and became difficult.</p><p>Eventually, when the river was no longer favorable as a center for trade and transportation, moving the town's administrative center was inevitable. Official reports state that Takua Pa had to move its town center to an area called Yan Yao in 1913.</p><p>That relocation marked the end of Talat Yai's prosperity as a center, turning this area into an old town stopped in time with the past. It left behind Sino-Portuguese architecture and traces of development for future generations to experience and study.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 ">Reasons for the town relocation are based on ONEP reports</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">Reading the Town Today</h2><p>Today, Takua Pa old town has come back to life in a different form from the past. The waterfront by the canal, which used to be a port for transporting tin, has now been revived into the area of the Takua Pa riverside market. It becomes lively again from Saturday afternoon to evening, filled with smiles, the aroma of local food, and the chatter of locals.</p><p>The governor's residence wall, once strong, is no longer used to defend against enemies. Instead, it has become a public space open to visitors and is often used as a venue for traditional festivals or major town events. Meanwhile, Wat Kongkha Phimuk continues to function as an active temple with residing monks. It is a peaceful place for meditation and a historical site that is still breathing.</p><p>If you have the chance to walk along the cultural street and the river, what surrounds you are not just stagnant ruins of the past, but evidence of adaptation for survival. The town that moved with the river back then continues to flow and change along with the people today.</p><p>Before fully believing any numbers or legends of the town, we would like to invite you to read and observe this town carefully. The history of Takua Pa is a charming large book. Some pages might be missing due to time, and some pages are still being written by all of us.</p></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takuapa Riverwalk</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="riverwalk">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Wat Kongkha Phimuk (the riverside temple)</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="wat-kongkha">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Governor’s Residence Wall</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="governor-wall">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The exact construction year of the governor's residence wall</li><li>Definitive evidence confirming the origin of Takua Pa's name</li><li>The reasons and builders of Wat Kongkha in its early days</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>Takua Pa Old Town</cite> · สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · Takua Pa Old Town webpage · <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Takua Pa Old Town Documents</cite> · สผ. (ONEP) · PDF pages 11-13, 55-56, 58 · <a href="https://nced.onep.go.th/opendata/2568/oldtown36_announce/takuapa.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Archaeological Site Database: Takua Pa Town</cite> · ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) · List of archaeological sites and suggested city walls · <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">The town's name originated from 'Takola' or 'Takua and Pa'</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">ONEP documents list both explanations, but emphasize that there is no definitive evidence to confirm either one. <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The governor's residence wall was built between 1840 and 1881</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">This is suggested, not settled, based on the tenure of Governor Phraya Senanuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon), and is not an exact year of construction. <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/874" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">Wat Kongkha Phimuk was built in 1762</div><div class="ev-status disputed">DISPUTED</div><div class="ev-explain">The cultural environment data states 1762, while the archaeological database uses 1936 as the construction year for the ordination hall. The age of the location and the remaining building might not align. <a href="https://archaeology.sac.or.th/archaeology/881" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">The Ongoing Debate Over the Town's Name</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">The Governor's Residence Wall and Town Defense</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">A Town That Grew with the River</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">The Day the River Grew Shallow and the Town Had to Move</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">Reading the Town Today</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
