s://takuapa101.com/stories/food-people/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/food-people/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/food-people/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/food-people/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/food-people/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/stories/food-people/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>People and flavors</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa Stories · People and Flavors</span><h1>Flavours of the Baba Community and the Old Market</h1><p class="lead">Food serves as both household memories and evidence of cultural exchange · Read Takua Pa through the Baba cuisine, double-layered Tao Sor, and the Culture Street</p><p class="story-meta">Updated 2026-09-07 · 7 min read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2 is-illustration"><img src="/assets/food-center.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Takua Pa Open-Air Food Center" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Takua Pa Open-Air Food Center</span><span class="credit">Illustration · No licensed photo available for this subject yet</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>A town that was once a tin mining hub and a port on the trans-peninsular route like Takua Pa has left behind more than just traces of Sino-Portuguese buildings or the operating hours of local markets. It also hides its own stories in the flavours and the way of life of its people.</p><p>In official documents, Takua Pa appears as a community that grew from integrating external cultures. Each dish acts as a reflection of settlement migration, cross-cultural marriages, and the changes in commercial districts through different eras. The flavours of today are edible memories.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">Takua Pa on the Baba Food Map</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/culture-street.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>When discussing the food culture on the Andaman coast, the Department of Cultural Promotion officially listed Takua Pa as part of the Baba-Peranakan food communities since its registration as a national cultural heritage in 2013 (2556 BE). This inclusion positions Takua Pa within the network of overseas Chinese descendants who arrived to mine tin and settled down with the locals.</p><p>This knowledge of flavours and cooking methods did not originate from any single isolated group. Instead, it emerged from passing down memories and culinary techniques within families. It is about preserving the identity and taste from generation to generation, whether it is a savoury dish in a regular meal or sweets for important family rituals.</p><p>However, placing Takua Pa on this food culture map serves to explain the overall cultural context. It does not mean that every household or shop in Takua Pa shares the exact same recipe for Moo Hong (braised pork belly), O-tao (fried oyster with taro), or Ang Ku (red tortoise cake). The diversity within each household remains the heart of the local food's vitality.</p></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Reading food menus or observing the local ingredients used in the market is a natural and gradual way of getting to know the way of life and flavours of Takua Pa.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">Double-layered Tao Sor and the Town's Transformation</h2><p>Among the frequently mentioned local foods, Tao Sor (flaky pastry) is a name that clearly appears in government records. The Phang Nga Provincial Tourism and Sports Office notes that Tao Sor is both a famous souvenir and an OTOP (One Tambon One Product) item of Takua Pa, describing it as similar to Chinese mooncakes but smaller, and often topped with sesame seeds.</p><p>The enduring presence of Tao Sor in Takua Pa was also recorded by the Food and Drug Administration during a field inspection in August 2025. It was documented that Tuang Rat shop continues the tradition of making Tao Sor using the traditional double-layered dough, an effort that earned them the FDA Quality Award for outstanding health products in the grassroots economy promotion category.</p><p>The story of Tao Sor helps us see how local sweets can develop and grow alongside the town's economy. At the same time, we cannot jump to the conclusion that Tao Sor originated in Takua Pa as the first in the world, or that there is only one monopolised original recipe here. This is because there is no historical evidence that clearly confirms its exact origin.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">Origin and original recipe are not settled</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">Culture Street and the Old Commercial District</h2><p>Stepping onto Si Takua Pa Road and Udom Thara Road, we are entering the former commercial centre of the tin mining era. Traces of this golden age are still visible through the architecture of shophouses with Straits colonial influences, featuring covered walkways at the front. Today, these streets have been repurposed into the Takua Pa Old Town Culture Street to draw people back to experience life in the old district.</p><p>The Department of Internal Trade's registry notes that this market is a gathering place for traditional sweets, local food, handicrafts, wickerwork, and cultural activities. The once-sleeping old streets are awakened every Sunday afternoon, creating a space where the younger generation and the original locals can meet and converse through shops and food.</p><p>However, the starting point of these activities still has information that is not entirely agreed upon. Some local news sources state that the retrospective event started in 2007, while official market registries list its founding and promotion in 2017. This discrepancy in information could be interpreted as referring to different milestones, between the community-initiated event and the official registration as a state-promoted market.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-disputed">Starting year of the event has conflicting sources</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">Lan Long Market, A Meeting Point of Diversity</h2><p>In addition to the walking street that opens once a week, Takua Pa also has a food hub ready to accommodate people every day, such as the Takua Pa Lan Long Food Centre. This place gathers various main dishes, ranging from rice and curry, made-to-order meals, Khanom Chin (fermented rice noodles), and noodles, to a complete selection of desserts and beverages.</p><p>These places are not just venues for buying and selling goods, but also serve as spaces for the townspeople to exchange conversations. Reading handwritten food menus or observing the local ingredients used in the market is a natural and gradual way of getting to know the way of life and flavours of Takua Pa.</p><p>As for information regarding the food centre's operating hours or the status of specific shops, data on the internet can be inaccurate and is not always updated in real-time. Asking locals on foot or checking directly with the vendors before your trip is a way to ensure you do not miss the chance to taste your favourite dishes.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">Operating hours and prices may change according to the season</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">Siwara Cafe in the Town's Storytelling System</h2><p>If you visit Takua Pa today, you might see many coffee shops and cafes attempting to present the town's story through their drinks and decor. Siwara Cafe is one of them, and is also the creator of this Takua Pa 101 information. The website has specifically categorised the cafe under the beverage shop section.</p><p>This categorisation and explanation are done so that readers can clearly, transparently, and without confusion separate the role of the information creators from historical facts. As for operating hours, new menus, or prices, readers can check the latest details directly through the shop's communication channels.</p><p>The growth of coffee shops and sitting areas today is a reflection of building upon the drinking and eating culture of the past. It blends with the lifestyles of the younger generation and locals who participate in revitalising the old district, bringing it back to life.</p></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takua Pa Open-Air Food Center</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="food-center">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="culture-street">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><aside class="story-outro"><p>This article tells the story behind the flavours. For a list of places that are still open, with opening hours and locations, see the food and travel guide, which the people behind this website visited and tasted for themselves</p><a class="text-link" href="https://siwaracafe.com/guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open the Takua Pa food and travel guide ↗</a></aside><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The exact person who invented the first Tao Sor recipe in Takua Pa cannot be confirmed by printed documents.</li><li>The exact and most updated operating hours of markets and shops today.</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>National Intangible Cultural Heritage: Baba Food</cite> · กรมส่งเสริมวัฒนธรรม (Department of Cultural Promotion) · Page 124 (print) / PDF page 133 · <a href="https://book.culture.go.th/newbook/ich/ich2013.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Field news on Phang Nga Tao Sor pastry products</cite> · สำนักงานคณะกรรมการอาหารและยา (Food and Drug Administration) · 2025 (2568 BE) field news · <a href="https://dis.fda.moph.go.th/detail-newsUpdate?id=3271" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Phang Nga Local Products</cite> · สำนักงานการท่องเที่ยวและกีฬาจังหวัดพังงา (Phang Nga Provincial Tourism and Sports Office) · Takua Pa Tao Sor pastry · <a href="https://phangnga.mots.go.th/news/369" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Thanon Watthanatham Mueang Kao Takua Pa</cite> · กรมการค้าภายใน (Department of Internal Trade) · History · <span class="source-offline">View sources <small>Original source offline · checked 8 Sep 2026</small></span> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Experience the Cultural Street</cite> · ผู้จัดการออนไลน์ (Manager Online) · News content referencing 2007 (2550 BE) · <a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9650000044214" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Lan Long Market</cite> · Wongnai · General information · <a href="https://www.wongnai.com/attractions/399190Vq-ตลาดลานโล่ง" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Lan Long Food Court</cite> · เทศบาลเมืองตะกั่วป่า (Takua Pa Town Municipality) · Video description Sep 1, 2024 (2567 BE) · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YBj0I_sMc-I" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">Tao Sor pastry originated in Takua Pa and has only one recipe</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">There is no historical evidence clearly stating the exact first origin or establishing a monopoly of a single recipe in Takua Pa. <a href="https://dis.fda.moph.go.th/detail-newsUpdate?id=3271" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The Culture Street event started in 2007 or 2017</div><div class="ev-status disputed">DISPUTED</div><div class="ev-explain">Some news sources cite 2007 for the beginning of the community's retrospective event, but the Department of Internal Trade documents state 2017 as a promoted market. <span class="source-offline">References ↗ <small>Original source offline · checked 8 Sep 2026</small></span></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">Takua Pa on the Baba Food Map</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">Double-layered Tao Sor and the Town's Transformation</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">Culture Street and the Old Commercial District</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">Lan Long Market, A Meeting Point of Diversity</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">Siwara Cafe in the Town's Storytelling System</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
D:\Siwaracafeweb\output\web\takuapa101-v1\site\en\stories\kuapapoh\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Houses Reopened · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover"><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Houses Reopened"><meta property="og:description" content="A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover"><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Takua Pa 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="Takua Pa 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/kuapapoh/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Kuapa Poh, an Old Takua Pa House Brought Back to Life", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/kuapapoh/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/kuapapoh/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/kuapapoh/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/stories/kuapapoh/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>City topics</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa Stories · Town Topics</span><h1>Kuapapoh: The Day Old Houses Stand Up to Tell Their Stories</h1><p class="lead">A creative space project that revived houses number 78, 109, and 305 · What the new generation tells about the town, and what history still needs to uncover</p><p class="story-meta">Updated on 2026-09-07 · 6-minute read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-brand.jpg" width="1076" height="1521" alt="Kuapapoh, the identity of the old town project" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Kuapapoh, the identity of the old town project</span><span class="credit">โครงการกั่วป่าโพ้ (The Kuapapoh project)</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>Imagine strolling along Sri Takua Pa Road, a street lined with old shophouses that have stood the test of time. Many of these buildings have their wooden doors quietly shut, sleeping away alongside the long-gone golden age of tin mining. Underneath that quietness, however, lie countless memories of the town, its people, and its trade, waiting to be told.</p><p>In recent years, a creative space project called 'Kuapapoh Ji Show Mueang' has stepped in to open the doors of these once-abandoned old houses and bring them back to life. They did not just repaint or repair the roofs, but are bringing the stories of Takua Pa back on display for the newer generations to see.</p><p>This movement reflects a town trying to stand up and tell its own story through the perspectives of locals, artists, and community collaboration. However, in reading these changes, we must still distinguish between newly created narratives for tourism and historical facts that still need careful verification.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">The Once Sleeping Spaces Return to the Town</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/shophouse-109.webp" width="400" height="533" alt="Shophouse number 109 on the old town culture street" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Shophouse number 109 on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><p>We often remember Takua Pa through its prosperous tin mining era. When that industry declined, many buildings were left behind. One of them is house number 78, formerly a mine owner's residence and a tin mining company office. Today, this once-empty space has been transformed into the 'Kuapa Mueang Museum', exhibiting mining equipment, old maps, and important documents for visitors to experience the traces of the past.</p><p>Not far away on the same route is house number 109, the building of the Kang Tang Yui Kuan or Wui Kun shrine, an association of the Hakka Chinese. The second floor of the building still houses the deity Tham Kung Ya for people to pay their respects. But the ground floor space, which used to be empty and unused, has been turned into the 'Kuapapoh Gallery' to host exhibitions and showcase the wisdom of the Takua Pa old town residents.</p><p>Another significant building that the project has dusted off is building number 305, or 'Tao Ming School' (the town's first Chinese school), which was an educational center during the prosperous mining era. The team came up with the idea to revive this space under the name 'Tao Ming: The Spark of Learning', using it as a venue to exhibit knowledge on architecture, culture, and traditions related to Takua Pa.</p><p>The effort to bring these three spaces back into use is an initiative filled with the project owners' determination to add economic value and create worth for the town. We must collectively understand, however, that this is an explanation and operation at the community project level, not an official declaration of historical site status or historical endorsement from government agencies.</p></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Working with community narratives is about balancing beautiful memories with facts that must be verified.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">Kuapapoh: A Moniker Carried by the River</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/tao-ming-school.jpg" width="1477" height="1108" alt="Tao Ming School seen from the front, on the old town culture street" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Tao Ming School seen from the front, on the old town culture street</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><p>As soon as you hear the name 'Kuapapoh', the first question that usually follows is where this word comes from. The project's archival information explains that this was the term Hokkien Chinese used in the past to call Takua Pa town, dividing the translation into two parts: 'Kuapa' meaning Takua Pa, and 'Poh' meaning market or port.</p><p>When combined, the word conveys 'Takua Pa port market', reflecting the past image of Chinese people sailing across the sea to trade and mine tin, using the pier as a center for exchanging both goods and culture. In addition, in the community's belief, the word Kuapapoh is also embedded in the rituals performed at various shrines during the vegetarian festival, becoming a word with deep meaning in the hearts of the townspeople.</p><p>While this is a beautiful story that connects well with the community's roots, in academic circles, the origin of the Takua Pa town name is still debated with several suggested, not settled hypotheses. Some refer to the ancient name Takola, while others base it on the words takua (lead) and pa (forest).</p><p>Therefore, the project's explanation is considered a record of memory and interpretation through the context of the Hokkien Chinese community, rather than a single, settled historical academic conclusion. This is the reason we must read this story with an open mind and caution.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">not settled academically</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">Peranakan Charm Through the Brush Tip</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/tao-ming.webp" width="1000" height="667" alt="Tao Ming School" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Tao Ming School</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The liveliness of the old town district reappeared concretely in September 2026 (2569 BE) when 78 Studio on Takua Pa Culture Street opened its space to host a solo art exhibition titled 'Peranakan Charm · Still Becoming'.</p><p>The artist behind these works is Tinnaphop Ngansathir, or Chang Pong. He is a native of Takua Pa old market, growing up with cartoon drawings, superheroes, and Chinese deities familiar in the local culture. After accumulating artistic experience for a long time, he chose to use his brush tip to convey the stories of the town he is attached to.</p><p>The artworks in the exhibition focus on extracting Peranakan colors, patterns, and clothing for display, serving as a bridge connecting the glorious past with people in the present era. Each painting is not just visually beautiful, but an emotional record of the artist towards the town.</p><p>Even though the project documents clearly record the exhibition hosting and the opening of space for artists to express themselves, what we still do not see in the reports are the number of attendees or a formal economic impact assessment. These activities thus serve as cultural ripples rather than official government statistics.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">Viewing the Town Through the Lens of Six Heritages</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-pattern.jpg" width="640" height="145" alt="Peranakan tile patterns still visible in the old town" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Peranakan tile patterns still visible in the old town</span><span class="credit">โครงการกั่วป่าโพ้ (The Kuapapoh project)</span></figcaption></figure><p>To help visitors understand the identity of Takua Pa old town more deeply, the project did not stop at organizing exhibitions. They also compiled and presented the '6 Or' concept to use as a framework for viewing the town's heritage hidden in daily life.</p><p>This concept starts with 'Aphon' (Apparel) or local clothing, whether it is lace fabrics, batik sarongs, and various accessories. Followed by 'Asai' (Abode) which covers the way of life, trading, and living in accordance with the times. As for 'Ahan' (Food), it is indispensable. It is the traditional flavors passed down from generation to generation, and the old restaurants that still keep their stoves on to this day.</p><p>In the dimension of the mind, the town is driven by 'Arom' (Emotion) expressed through art, music, and the sound of drums in the vegetarian festival. Meanwhile, 'Aram' (Temples and Shrines) is the center of faith, including temples, shrines, and Yui Kuan scattered around the old town district. And finally, 'Akhan' (Architecture), the structures that stand as witnesses to change.</p><p>All these concepts were also further developed in the design of souvenirs, such as the Poh Shop project's t-shirts. The revenue from the sales is brought back to fund the restoration and drive the town's activities forward, truly circulating value back to the community.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">What the Town Tells and What We Must Verify</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/culture-street.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Kuapapoh project allows us to see the determination of the new generation and locals who want to preserve and build upon the stories of Takua Pa. They created creative spaces, brought in art as a medium, and turned once-closed houses into new travel destinations.</p><p>What is recorded on the project's website and documents serves as a contemporary archive that the community chose to tell about itself. It is a memory full of life and the pride of the residents.</p><p>At the same time, however, reading these stories also calls for us to maintain a balance between appreciation and historical facts. Many parts of the information are explanations that have not been endorsed by government agencies and cannot replace calendars or academic documents.</p><p>This is the challenge and fun of walking around Takua Pa old town today. We have the task of listening to those beautiful stories while simultaneously asking questions and finding answers from reliable sources, so that our journey does not end with just looking at pictures, but truly understanding the town in all dimensions.</p></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Tao Ming School</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="tao-ming">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="culture-street">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takuapa Riverwalk</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="riverwalk">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>The number of attendees or the official economic impact generated by the project</li><li>The legal status or endorsement from government agencies of the three houses</li><li>The clear and settled origin of the Takua Pa town name in history</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>Kuapapoh · Project Archives</cite> · Kuapapoh Project · content.json · origin, events, openings sections · <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">Defining houses number 78, 109, and 305 as creative spaces</div><div class="ev-status qualified">QUALIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">This is an explanation from the project owner, not a declaration of historical site or space status from a government agency. <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The origin of the word Kuapapoh translates to Takua Pa port market</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">The origin of the Takua Pa town name in historical work still has several suggested, not settled hypotheses, thus the project's explanation should not be used as a settled academic conclusion. <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">The hosting of the Peranakan Charm exhibition by Tinnaphop Ngansathir in September 2026 (2569 BE)</div><div class="ev-status qualified">QUALIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">Referenced from project data, but not a retrospective official calendar confirming attendee numbers or economic impact. <a href="https://kuapapoh.pages.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">The Once Sleeping Spaces Return to the Town</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">Kuapapoh: A Moniker Carried by the River</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">Peranakan Charm Through the Brush Tip</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">Viewing the Town Through the Lens of Six Heritages</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">What the Town Tells and What We Must Verify</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div><section class="research-section" id="project-gallery"><h2>Images from Kuapapoh Project Archive</h2><div data-gallery><div class="gallery-tabs" hidden><button type="button" data-tab="today">Present photos</button><button type="button" data-tab="archive">Historical photos</button></div><div id="gallery-today" data-panel="today"><h3>Present photos</h3><div class="photo-grid"><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-brand.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · กั่วป่าโพ้ แบรนด์เมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-brand.jpg" width="1076" height="1521" alt="Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Town Brand" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Kuapapoh: Takua Pa Old Town Brand<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-pattern.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · ลายกระเบื้องเปอรานากันเมืองเก่า"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-pattern.jpg" width="640" height="145" alt="Old Town Peranakan Tile Patterns" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Old Town Peranakan Tile Patterns<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure></div></div><div id="gallery-archive" data-panel="archive"><h3>Historical photos</h3><div class="photo-grid"><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-history.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · แผ่นเล่าประวัติกั่วป่าโพ้"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-history.jpg" width="1078" height="1519" alt="Kuapapoh History Board" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Kuapapoh History Board<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-wuikun.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · หวุ่ยกุ่น"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-wuikun.jpg" width="1086" height="1448" alt="Wui Kun" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Wui Kun<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-1.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 1"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-1.jpg" width="900" height="1272" alt="Takua Pa Old Document 1" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Takua Pa Old Document 1<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure><figure class="research-photo"><a data-lightbox href="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-2.jpg" aria-label="ขยายภาพ · เอกสารเก่าเมืองตะกั่วป่า 2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-doc-2.jpg" width="900" height="1268" alt="Takua Pa Old Document 2" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>Takua Pa Old Document 2<small>Credit · Kua Pa Pho / Takua Pa Old Town Project image archive</small><small>License · Image from the project archive, cleared for this website</small></figcaption></figure></div></div></div></section></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
D:\Siwaracafeweb\output\web\takuapa101-v1\site\en\stories\water-trade\index.html:1:<!doctype html><html lang="en" data-base="" data-news-api=""><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>Thung Tuek and the Takua Pa River Trade · Takua Pa 101</title><meta name="description" content="The river is not just a backdrop. It is a route, a connector of people, and the determiner of the town&#x27;s fate, which had to relocate due to sedimentation."><meta name="robots" content="index,follow,max-image-preview:large"><meta property="og:title" content="Thung Tuek and the Takua Pa River Trade"><meta property="og:description" content="The river is not just a backdrop. It is a route, a connector of people, and the determiner of the town&#x27;s fate, which had to relocate due to sedimentation."><meta property="og:url" content="https://takuapa101.com/stories/water-trade/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta property="og:site_name" content="Takua Pa 101"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://takuapa101.com/assets/og/default.png"><meta name="generator" content="Takua Pa 101"><link rel="canonical" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/water-trade/"><link rel="preload" href="/assets/NotoSerifThai.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="preload" href="/assets/IBMPlexSansThaiLooped-Regular.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/design.css"><script type="application/ld+json">[{"@context": "https://schema.org", "inLanguage": "en", "@type": "BreadcrumbList", "itemListElement": [{"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/"}, {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Thung Tuek and the Takua Pa River, an Ancient Trade Route", "item": "https://takuapa101.com/stories/water-trade/"}]}]</script><script src="/assets/design.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/trip.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/ui.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/theme.css"><script src="/assets/theme.js"></script><script src="/assets/search.js" defer></script><script src="/assets/live-news.js" defer></script><meta name="theme-color" content="#203f39"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="th" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/water-trade/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="en" href="https://takuapa101.com/en/stories/water-trade/"><link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="https://takuapa101.com/stories/water-trade/"></head><body class="inner-page"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="/en/" aria-label="Takua Pa 101 Home"><span class="brand-mark">101</span><span>Takua Pa<small>TAKUA PA FIELD NOTES</small></span></a><nav id="main-nav" aria-label="เมนูหลัก"><a href="/en/places/">Places</a><a href="/en/stories/" aria-current="page">Stories</a><a href="/en/map/">Map</a><a href="/en/rest/">Eat and explore</a><a href="/en/news/">Updates</a><a href="/en/trip/">Your trip</a><a href="/en/traditions/" class="nav-more">Traditions</a><a href="/en/eat/" class="nav-more">Eat & Souvenirs</a><a href="/en/routes/" class="nav-more">Walking Routes</a><a href="/en/leaflet/" class="nav-more">Leaflet</a><a href="/en/about/" class="nav-more">About</a></nav><div class="header-tools"><a class="lang-switch" href="/stories/water-trade/" hreflang="th" lang="th">ไทย</a><a class="lang-switch" href="/en/stories/water-trade/" hreflang="en" lang="en">EN</a><button class="theme-toggle" type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-pressed="false" aria-label="สลับเป็นโหมดกลางคืน"><span class="tt-night" aria-hidden="true">☾</span><span class="tt-day" aria-hidden="true">☀</span></button><button class="nav-toggle" type="button" data-nav-toggle aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="main-nav" aria-label="Open menu"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">☰</span></button><button class="icon-button" type="button" data-open-search aria-label="Search website (Press Ctrl+K)"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌕</span></button><a class="icon-button" href="/en/trip/" aria-label="Your trip"><span class="ib-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span><span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a><a class="header-map" href="/en/map/">Open map ↗</a></div></header><main id="main"><div class="story-article-v2"><header class="story-hero-v2"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa Stories</a><span>/</span><span>Waterways and trade</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa Stories · River and Trade</span><h1>From Thung Tuk to the Takua Pa River: The Day the Waterway Built the Town</h1><p class="lead">The river is not just a backdrop. It is a route, a connector of people, and the determiner of the town's fate, which had to relocate due to sedimentation.</p><p class="story-meta">Updated 2026-09-07 · 7 min read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/photos/kuapapoh-port.webp" width="264" height="282" alt="Archive photograph: boats moored at the Takua Pa landing" loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Archive photograph: boats moored at the Takua Pa landing</span><span class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>If you stand by the Takua Pa River today, the slow and quiet water might seem like a mere backdrop to the old town landscape. However, looking back into history, this river never slept. It was the main artery that pumped prosperity, people, and resources into the area for centuries.</p><p>Takua Pa town was not located at its current coordinates from the beginning. It shifted and grew along the waterway, moving from the flatland near the estuary to the Talat Yai area, until the day river sediment forced the town centre to relocate once again.</p><p>The Takua Pa River is therefore not just a natural landscape, but the true author of history that brought global maritime commerce to the area, and the determiner of the town's destiny when the waterway became shallow.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">Thung Tuk, the Port Before the Town</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/riverwalk.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Takuapa Riverwalk" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Takuapa Riverwalk</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>Before the image of Takua Pa town became the brick buildings and Sino-Portuguese commercial districts we see today, the original trade centre of this area was located at Thung Tuk, a flatland near the river mouth. Thung Tuk served as a bustling ancient trading port on the trans-peninsular route.</p><p>This estuary area did not grow in isolation. It was a crucial piece connecting to an extensive maritime trade network. It served as a stopover and goods exchange point for travellers of various nationalities, who relied on ocean currents and seasonal monsoons for navigation.</p><p>The Takua Pa waterway during that era was not solely for local transportation. It was used as a primary trans-peninsular route between the Andaman Sea and the Gulf of Thailand. With a favourable distance between the headwaters on both sides, this route drastically reduced travel time.</p><p>Importantly, using the river route helped navigators avoid the dangers and risks of sailing around the long Strait of Malacca. The waterway therefore acted as a bridge connecting two oceans, continuously bringing people, goods, and cultures into the area. This was the first chapter of the port town, before the centre of prosperity expanded deeper inland along the river.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">Suggested, not settled status of the ancient port</span></div></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Takua Pa town was not located at its current coordinates from the beginning. It shifted and grew along the waterway.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-1">The Steam Boiler and the Prosperity of Talat Yai</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/iron-bridge.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Boon Soong Iron Bridge" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Boon Soong Iron Bridge</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>As eras transitioned and maritime trade moved further inland, the town centre relocated to the Talat Yai community during the town's restoration in the reign of King Rama III. This area was surrounded by the river on three sides, making it highly suitable for both town defence and water transportation.</p><p>Takua Pa's water trade continued to flourish and left material traces visible to this day. If you travel to Wat Pathum Thara, or Wat Na Mueang (the town's front temple), in the Talat Yai area, you will find a large steam boiler on display. This metal piece is not mining machinery, but a crucial component of a historical steamboat.</p><p>Phraya Sena Nuchit (Nuch Na Nakhon) partnered with Phraya Wichit Songkhram to purchase this steamboat in 1867 (2410 B.E.) to transport goods and suppress pirates in the waters. It serves as proof of the wealth and heavy traffic volume during that era.</p><p>Although the steamboat later deteriorated and sank in the Takua Pa River, the salvaged steam boiler still stands as a witness. The vibrancy of the Talat Yai community at that time was inextricably linked to the river. The people's way of life relied on the waterway, which functioned as the town's main street.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">The temple's role in trade</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">The Day Sediment Accumulated and the Town Had to Move</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/wat-pathum.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>But the river is never static, and nature always plays a role in shaping the town's fate. Over time, sediment from the rapidly growing tin mining industry and natural phenomena accumulated until the Takua Pa River became increasingly shallow.</p><p>This geographical change directly impacted the town's economic structure. Navigation, once convenient, decreased and became more difficult. Large merchant ships that previously brought wealth could no longer dock as easily as before, causing the vibrancy of the Talat Yai community to decline.</p><p>Eventually, the town centre had to relocate from the original Talat Yai area to a new district called Yan Yao in 1913. This relocation was not caused by war or epidemics, but by the limitations of the changed waterway, which was no longer conducive to trade.</p><p>This historical event proves that the river had complete influence over human settlement. The day the water became shallow was the day the town had to pack its bags and move to breathe new life into an area better able to accommodate the changes.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">Date and cause of the town relocation</span></div></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">Boonsoong Iron Bridge, the Miners' Connection</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/thung-phra.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="Thung Phra Pho Park" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Thung Phra Pho Park</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>As the town centre transitioned from the commercial port era to the golden age of tin mining, the riverside landscape and infrastructure were modified accordingly. In the past, the Takua Pa Canal was over forty metres wide, presenting a natural barrier that divided the area.</p><p>The width of the waterway meant miners and locals had to row boats back and forth between the old market side and the mining company's area. To solve this difficult commute, especially during the flood season when the current was swift, Mr Juti Boonsoong, founder of the tin dredging company, initiated the construction of a wooden bridge first, allowing people to walk and ride bicycles across.</p><p>Later in 1968, the bridge underwent a major renovation. Steel from decommissioned tin dredges was assembled to rebuild the Khok Khanun Iron Bridge, or as we familiarly know it, the Boonsoong Iron Bridge, spanning over two hundred metres across the river.</p><p>This iron bridge is therefore not just a construction object or an engineering structure. It is a shortcut that reduced travel time for workers, a goods transport route, and a connection for daily life. This landmark tells the story of the transition from the rowboat era to the mining industry era that powerfully sustained the town's economy.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-4">Water Catchment and Merit-Making at Thung Phra Pho</h2><p>The Takua Pa River not only sustains the economy but is also tied to daily life and how locals cope with nature. Riverside areas like Thung Phra Pho Public Park are clear examples of adapting to coexist with the old town's water system.</p><p>Municipal planning documents indicate that the Thung Phra Pho area is important as water management infrastructure, with projects to dredge the canal and construct a pier. During times when the town faces flooding, this area is also used as an installation point for water pumps to alleviate floods for the community.</p><p>However, beyond the dimension of dealing with natural disasters, Thung Phra Pho is also an open space for cultural activities. On the full moon day of the twelfth lunar month every year, this wide riverside plaza is used as the venue for the municipality's annual Loy Krathong (November lantern-floating festival).</p><p>This diverse usage reflects that the riverside areas were not abandoned after the mining era. Instead, their functions were adapted to become both a town defence mechanism and an activity plaza that brings people together on merit-making days.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-5">Traces Still Flowing in the Present Landscape</h2><p>Today, although the mines have closed and large-scale maritime trade has become merely a past recorded on paper pages, many riverside locations are still alive and harmoniously coexist with the breath of the people in Takua Pa old town.</p><p>The Boonsoong Iron Bridge continues to serve as a community thoroughfare, where locals still walk and ride motorcycles back and forth. At the same time, it has become a landmark where people come to enjoy the view and take photos of the old dredge factory traces still visible from the bridge, or even being used as a filming location for foreign movies.</p><p>Along the canal near Wat Yan Yao (a local temple), a community market area named Takua Pa Riverside Market has been initiated to help revive the old atmosphere, allowing people to experience the riverside way of life once again. There are also seafood festivals held according to the cultural calendar.</p><p>The Takua Pa River continues to flow slowly. It is a guardian of memories from the days when it was a global port town crowded with merchant ships, to the day it became the tranquil viewpoint of the old town we see today.</p></section><section class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Takuapa Riverwalk</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="riverwalk">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Boon Soong Iron Bridge</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="iron-bridge">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Wat Pathum Thara (the front town temple)</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="wat-pathum">Add to trip</button></div></div><div class="place-card-mini"><div><h3>Thung Phra Pho Park</h3><button class="button button-outline" data-trip-add="thung-phra">Add to trip</button></div></div></div></section><details class="unknowns-box-v2"><summary>Missing data</summary><ul><li>Architectural characteristics and town layout details of the Thung Tuk port in the era prior to the tin mining trade</li><li>Contemporary photographs with clear usage rights depicting the congestion of water traffic during the era when steamboats were still operating</li><li>Documents officially recording the day of the relocation to Yan Yao, and the voices of the villagers in the Talat Yai community at that time</li></ul></details><section class="story-sources-v2"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li><cite>Thung Tuek Ancient Trading Port</cite> · กรมศิลปากร (Fine Arts Department) · Thung Tuek archaeological site article · <a href="https://www.finearts.go.th/architecture/view/35708-ทุ่งตึกเมืองท่าการค้าโบราณ" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Takua Pa Old Town Documents</cite> · สำนักงานนโยบายและแผนทรัพยากรธรรมชาติและสิ่งแวดล้อม (Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and Planning) · PDF pages 11-13, 55-56, 58 · <a href="https://nced.onep.go.th/opendata/2568/oldtown36_announce/takuapa.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Archaeological Site Database: Talat Yai Community</cite> · ศูนย์มานุษยวิทยาสิรินธร (Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre) · Community historical data · <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Bun Sung Iron Bridge (Khok Khanun)</cite> · ฐานข้อมูลท้องถิ่นภาคใต้ มหาวิทยาลัยสงขลานครินทร์ (Southern Local Database, Prince of Songkla University) · History and overview page · <a href="https://clib.psu.ac.th/southerninfo/content/1/de645890" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Environmental Action Plan Document 2022 (2565 BE)</cite> · Thetsaban Mueang Takua Pa (Takua Pa Town Municipality) · Project Records and Water Management · <a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/files/com_strategy/2022-12_231dcd9d5169573.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Takua Pa Flood News and Water Pump Installation</cite> · MGR Online · Regional News · <a href="https://mgronline.com/south/detail/9560000096203" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Takua Pa Riverside Market, Ruamjai Pracharath</cite> · Krom Kan Kha Phainai Krasuang Phanit (Department of Internal Trade, Ministry of Commerce) · Must-Visit Market Information · <span class="source-offline">View sources <small>Original source offline · checked 8 Sep 2026</small></span> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Morning News: Boon Sung Iron Bridge</cite> · NBT2HD · News Program 30 January 2026 (2569) · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QUZ54DTc0LQ" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li><li><cite>Seafood Festival Calendar, Takua Pa Riverside Market</cite> · Krasuang Watthanatham (Ministry of Culture) · Cultural Activity Calendar · <a href="https://calendar.m-culture.go.th/events/103651" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View sources</a> (Accessed 2026-09-07)</li></ol></section></div><div class="story-sidebar-v2"><aside class="evidence-box-v2"><h3>Where is the evidence?</h3><ul class="ev-items-v2"><li><div class="ev-claim">Trans-peninsular distance and the origin of Thung Tuk</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">The distance figures are data from the ONEP report, and the establishment of Thung Tuk refers to the Fine Arts Department's explanation. The website maintains the level of the text according to the original documents and has not verified new conclusions. <a href="https://www.finearts.go.th/architecture/view/35708-ทุ่งตึกเมืองท่าการค้าโบราณ" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">Town relocation due to the shallow river</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">The date and reason for the town's relocation in 1913 are compiled data based on the ONEP report, which should be cited from the original source report when used in academic work. <a href="https://culturalenvi.onep.go.th/site/detail/4428" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li><li><div class="ev-claim">Origin of the steam boiler at Wat Na Mueang</div><div class="ev-status unverified">UNVERIFIED</div><div class="ev-explain">Information from the Princess Maha Chakri Sirindhorn Anthropology Centre database indicates the purchase of the steamboat in 1867, but this piece of evidence does not verify that the temple operated the mining business directly. <a href="https://wikicommunity.sac.or.th/community/1527" target="_blank" rel="noopener">References ↗</a></div></li></ul></aside><nav class="story-toc-v2 scrollspy" id="story-toc"><h2>In this article</h2><ul class="scrollspy-nav"><li><a href="#section-0">Thung Tuk, the Port Before the Town</a></li><li><a href="#section-1">The Steam Boiler and the Prosperity of Talat Yai</a></li><li><a href="#section-2">The Day Sediment Accumulated and the Town Had to Move</a></li><li><a href="#section-3">Boonsoong Iron Bridge, the Miners' Connection</a></li><li><a href="#section-4">Water Catchment and Merit-Making at Thung Phra Pho</a></li><li><a href="#section-5">Traces Still Flowing in the Present Landscape</a></li></ul></nav></div></div></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="footer-top"><a href="/en/" class="footer-name">Takua Pa <em>101</em></a><p>One city<br>Stories to get to know</p><a class="round-link" href="#main" aria-label="Back to top">↑</a></div><div class="footer-bottom"><a href="https://siwaracafe.com/">Created by Baan Siwara, Takua Pa</a><span>Old Town Guide · Phang Nga Province</span><a href="https://www.takuapacity.go.th/pdf/travel-preview.pdf">Original Brochure ↗</a></div></footer><nav class="tabbar" aria-label="Quick menu"><a href="/en/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">⌂</span>Home</a><a href="/en/map/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◈</span>Map</a><a href="/en/eat/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>Eat</a><a href="/en/trip/"><span class="tb-glyph" aria-hidden="true">◫</span>Trip<span class="qs-badge" data-trip-count hidden>0</span></a></nav></body></html>
